    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "342d6ebb_9c669e6b",
        "filename": "src/capture/SkCaptureManager.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 20,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-25T17:17:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is going to leak and run into double free issues for a couple of reasons:\n1. Nothing in SkCaptureCanvas keeps the `canvas` alive after it wraps it. This could be addressed by SkCaptureCanvas taking ownership with its `fBaseCanvas` field changing to `std::unique_ptr\u003cSkCanvas\u003e`.  Right now, when SkSurface_Base calls makeCaptureCanvas, `baseCanvas will be deleted as soon as getCachedCanvas() returns.\n\n2. The returned SkCaptureCanvas object is being wrapped in a unique pointer twice, once here to be stored in `fTrackedCanvasses`, but again inside `getCachedCanvas()`.  If the surface is deleted, it\u0027ll delete the SkCaptureCanvas and then the capture manager will be accessing a dead pointer.\n\nIt\u0027s pretty straightforward to work out the ownership for the base canvas going into the SkCaptureCanvas.  I also think that it makes sense for the capture manager to be the owner of the SkCaptureCanvases (and not the SkSurface, although this gets tricky).  \n\nWe can have SkSurface_Base split its fCachedCanvas into a bare pointer, which is always set to either the base canvas or the captured canvas, and a separate fOwnedCanvas that is either the base canvas (for non-capture) or null (for when the SkCaptureCanvas owns the base canvas and the capture manager owns the capture canvas).\n\nInstead of having SkCaptureCanvas own its base canvas, we could keep it holding a bare canvas, and then have SkSurface_Base\u0027s fOwnedCanvas always keep the base canvas alive even if fCachedCanvas is a capture canvas.  However, we then need to be able to signal back to the capture manager when the surface is deleted so that the capture canvas can be unlinked from the now-deleted base canvas (and also this is probably a good event to be tracking in the SkCapture anyways).",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 18,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 20,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "2a1a838f87c71f11ee6476c5c8e7b74b12759486",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a88222b2_35188729",
        "filename": "src/capture/SkCaptureManager.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 5
